<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml">
  <title>Identity</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:title>Identity</md:title>
    <md:content-id>m00007</md:content-id>
    <md:uuid>e1607dd4-ec88-42a4-af9c-7a8ec705de82</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="learning-objectives" id="sect-00001">
      <title>Learning Objectives</title>
      <para id="para-00002">By the end of this section, you will be able to</para>
        <list id="list-00001">
          <item>Explain the psychological factors at work in trauma</item>
          <item>Explain the psychological factors at work in resiliency</item>
          <item>Identify the major health concerns of infancy</item>
        </list>
    </section>
    <para id="para-00001">
      Patrice is exhausted. Whenever she attempts to leave her son Marco in his playpen so that she can do housework, he erupts in a wail of cries. It breaks her heart to make her toddler so upset, but she knows that he is safe, and that chores need to be done. Yet even when he is content and happily playing, the sight of his mother leaving the room causes Marco to panic.
    </para>
    <para id="para-00005">
      Patrice worries about the future and what will happen when Marco starts to attend <term class="no-emphasis" id="term-00001">childcare</term> outside the home in a few months. She expects that Marco will be upset everyday when she leaves him at the daycare center. She’s not sure what to do to alleviate the distress that he experiences and she feels immense guilt for needing to leave her baby. Although very upsetting, what Patrice is experiencing is typical and may actually be signs of a healthy infant and secure infant-caregiver attachment. We will learn about this in the next section.
    </para>
    <note class="life-hacks" id="note-00001">
      <title>Understanding Temper Tantrums</title>
      <para id="para-00006">
        Toddlers are often seeking autonomy, or control over themselves, and tantrums can erupt when they feel forced to go to bed, go to the car, or eat their food and they have other priorities. Giving a toddler a sense of choice, even if it’s false choice can help them feel better. For instance, if a toddler is resisting getting dressed, you can ask if they’d like to put on their left or right sock first. This sense of choice gives toddlers a sense of autonomy so they are less likely to have a tantrum. Allowing toddlers to try to do things for themselves, like feeding, dressing, and tidying toys can also help to give them a sense of autonomy that can alleviate a tantrum (<link target-id="Figure12_05_children"/>).
      </para>
      <figure id="Figure12_05_children">
        <media alt="A map shows Tenochtitlan.">
          <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Figure12_05_children.png"/>
        </media>
          <caption>At its height, Tenochtitlan was one of the largest cities in the world, with a population of up to 200,000. After the conquest of the Aztec empire, the Spanish appropriated this floating city as their capital. Note the flag of Imperial Spain at the top left of the island city.</caption>
      </figure>
      <para id="para-00009">
        When a temper tantrum occurs (and they will), stay calm. Some toddlers prefer to have cuddles to help calm them, but others prefer to not be touched as they feel overstimulated. Make sure they are physically safe and not near any hazards and give them time to cry and release their emotions. If they are crying because they want something that they should not have, do not reward them and let them have it, but you can reward them with something else once they are calm. Most importantly, try not to scream or get agitated yourself, this will only escalate things and make the tantrum last longer as shown in <link target-id="Figure09_04_Adolescent"/>.
      </para>
      <figure id="Figure09_04_Adolescent">
        <media alt="Alt text placeholder.">
          <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Figure09_04_Adolescent.png"/>
        </media>
          <caption>Figure title. Figure caption goes here.</caption>
      </figure>
      <para id="para-00010">
        In the early years, much of infant and toddler behavior appears biologically influenced. Temperament, arousal, emotions, and regulation all have strong biological components. But in this section, which will explore how the social world of infants supports their emotional development and growth.
      </para>
    </note>

    <para id="para-00011">
      Infants require social interaction to grow and thrive. From the earliest age, infants benefit from being held, spoken to, and loved. Without constant social feedback from parents and caregivers, infants would struggle to understand and navigate the world around themselves.
    </para>
    <para id="para-00012">
      Shortly after birth, infants begin to look towards and attend to the voice of their biological mother. This voice is most familiar to them and provides comfort and reassurance. As infants look towards voices, they begin to understand faces, and at approximately 2 months of age infants will produce their first social smile which is a reactive smile they make in response to seeing another smiling face. Social smiles may not represent an infant’s actual mood or emotion, but allows them to bond with their caregivers by mirroring their expression.
    </para>
    <section id="sect-00008">
      <title>[H2] Imitation</title>
      <para id="para-00013">Involuntary <term id="term-00002">imitation</term> is an important <term id="term-00003">bonding mechanism</term> for young infants. Andrew <term id="term-00004">Meltzhoff</term> examined infant involuntary imitation which is the reflexive instinct for infants to match caregivers’ facial expressions. Young infants between the ages of 1 and 4 months have no voluntary control over this form of imitation. Instead, the mirror neurons in the infants’ brain allows them to replicate a variety of facial expressions such as smiles, smirks, and tongue protrusions. Through mirroring and matching a caregiver, involuntary imitation strengthens emotional bonds.</para>
      <para id="para-00014">
        By the age of 8 to 12 months, infants can display <term id="term-00005">voluntary imitation</term> in which they can imitate the gestures and facial expression of another person. Voluntary imitation is conscious and controllable, but limited to only immediate imitation at this age. A one year old infant is unable to imitate something they saw hours or days in the past.
      </para>
      <para id="para-00015">
        Along with voluntary imitation, one year old infants also display social referencing. Social referencing is the tendency to look to others during novel situations to understand how to feel and respond. In unusual or ambiguous situations, infants tend to look to the facial expression of their caregiver to interpret and respond to the experience. If a caregiver looks calm or happy, infants are more likely to interpret a novel situation as safe and exciting. If a caregiver looks nervous, upset, fearful, or angry, infants are more likely to become nervous, timid, or avoidant as shown in <link target-id="Figure09_02_Stages"/>.
      </para>
      <figure id="Figure09_02_Stages">
        <media alt="Alt text placeholder">
          <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Figure09_02_Stages.png"/>
        </media>
          <caption>In less than thirty years, the thirteen colonies went from being an outpost of the British empire to a sovereign republican nation based on ideals of liberty and self-government.</caption>
      </figure>
      <para id="para-00016">
        Social referencing can be especially helpful during times of <term id="term-00006">joint attention</term>, or when an infant followers a caregiver’s body language or voice to attend to an intended stimuli. When a caregiver points to a toy or an animal, an infant is able to understand and follow their caregiver’s gaze and gesture. Joint attention requires the ability to understand the perspective of another person, which is the basis of <term id="term-00007">social cognition</term>, or understanding how or what others are thinking.
      </para>
      <para id="para-00017">
        Over the first year of life, infants develop voluntary imitation, social referencing, and joint attention skills through constant attention and focus on their caregivers. The role of the caregiver is essential to healthy social development of infants and toddlers, particularly as it comes to infant attachment.
      </para>
      <note class="it-depends" id="note-00002">
        <title>Cosleeping &amp; Attachment</title>
        <para id="para-00018">
          In many cultures around the world, cosleeping with infants, and sharing a bed or mattress with an infant is common place. In Africa, Asia, and Latin America, cosleeping is seen a beneficial way to bond with an infant, keep them safe, comfortable, and happy, and ensure the whole family receives a good night of sleep. <link target-id="Figure14_01_Adaptation"/> shows in European cultures, cosleeping has been less common over the past few centuries, with infants being placed in separate bassinets or cribs.
        </para>
        <figure id="Figure14_01_Adaptation">
          <media alt="A collage of faces of different ethnicities around a globe. Inside the globe is a scene of men standing behind a circular desk. Behind them are multiple different flags. The poster reads “We the peoples of the United Nations determined to save succeeding generations from the scourge of war . . . Preamble of the charter, signed June 26, 1945.”">
            <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Figure14_01_Adaptation.png"/>
          </media>
            <caption>This poster includes people from around the world and states the preamble of the United Nations Charter, “We the peoples of the United Nations, determined to save succeeding generations from the scourge of war . . . ”</caption>
        </figure>
        <para id="para-00019">
          Public health agencies in Canada and the United States often warn about the dangers of cosleeping, that a caregiver could accidently smother an infant with a blanket, roll onto the infant, or push them out of bed. There’s also an increased chance of Sudden Infant Death Syndrome, known as SIDS when families in North America cosleep (see <link target-id="Figure14_02_Moving"/>). Official health guidelines suggest that infants should be placed in their own crib, in the same room as their caregiver’s bed. The infant’s crib should not have any blankets, toys, or choking hazards. These guidelines can make bedtime routines tough, as many infants and toddlers experience separation anxiety and feel lonely in their own crib.
        </para>
        <figure id="Figure14_02_Moving">
          <media alt="Secretary of State Dean Acheson sits at a desk on a stage, signing the North Atlantic Treaty. Three men stand around him behind the desk. They face a crowd sitting in pews.">
            <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Figure14_01_Adaptation.png"/>
          </media>
            <caption>Figure title. U.S. Secretary of State Dean Acheson, along with the foreign ministers of Canada and 10 European nations, gathered to sign the North Atlantic Treaty on April 4, 1949, founding NATO.</caption>
        </figure>
        <para id="para-00020">
          In recent decades, some North American families are pivoting away from a separate crib and towards cosleeping. Adults who do not smoke, and have not ingested alcohol or drugs (including prescription sleeping pills) may have a lowered risk of infant suffocation as compared to adults who have ingested alcohol or drugs. Although parents of cosleeping infants report less sleep disturbance, research has actually found the opposite – that toddlers who cosleep often have more sleep disturbances (Yang et al., 2022).
        </para>
      </note>
      <para id="para-00021">
        Merchandising firms are extremely cost sensitive since they must not only pass the cost of goods on to the consumer but also earn a profit. Large merchandising firms manage these costs by buying in bulk, and negotiating with wholesalers and suppliers to drive per unit cost as low as possible. At the same time they seek to drive costs down, they must also manage their selling and administrative expenses. In recent years, large retailers have reduced cost by introducing labor-saving features such as self-check-out to reduce their labor costs.
      </para>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00009">
      <title>[H2]Subtypes of Attachment </title>
      <para id="para-00022">
        Over the course of the <term class="no-emphasis" id="term-00008">Strange Situation</term> paradigm, infants and caregivers are separated and reunited twice. During the first separation, a stranger is present to help provide comfort. During the second separation, the infant is momentarily left alone while being observed through a one-way mirror or through a video camera. During this second separation, the stranger returns first and attempts to comfort the infant before the caregiver returns the final time.
      </para>
      <para id="para-00023">
        When <term class="no-emphasis" id="term-00009">Ainsworth</term> first conducted the Strange Situation in North America, she found that nearly 65% of infants responded in a similar way. These infants engaged in social referencing behaviors and glanced back at caregivers during the first few steps of the paradigm. Then, they would become upset and cry during the separations, but would become calm, happy, and excited to see their primary caregiver again during each reunion. Ainsworth believed that these infants felt safe and secure as long as their caregiver was present, and she named this pattern of behaviors secure attachment. This is a bulleted list
        <list id="list-00002">
          <item>This is the first bullet</item>
          <item>This is the second bullet</item>
        </list>
      </para>
      <note class="intersections-contexts" id="note-00003">
        <title>Feature Boxes Have Titles Specific to Their Topic</title>
        <para id="para-00026">
          Infants and toddlers can be demanding and require around the clock care. New parents are often exhausted from sleepless nights, crying babies, and new challenges. For women who are recovering from birth, there are additional challenges of needing to physically heal while caring for a newborn.
        </para>
        <para id="para-00027">
          In addition to the physical scars of birth, many new mothers experience postpartum mental health concerns. Over 20% of new mothers may experience postpartum anxiety, and 16% may experience postpartum depression. These concerns are often brought on by the change in hormones from before to shortly after birth, and impacted by the stress new mothers face.</para>
        <para id="para-00028">
          Maternal mental health can impact the quality of care an infant receives. Struggling with elevated levels of anxiety and depression is difficult, and mothers experiencing mental health symptoms may smile, talk, and play with their babies less, respond to nonverbal cues less, and become more frustrated when infants have outbursts or refuse to calm. Long-term maternal health obstacles can impact infant-caregiver attachment and emotion regulation in toddlers. Therefore, it is important to recognize the signs and symptoms of postpartum anxiety and depression, and for family members to offer support for new mothers experiencing these conditions.
        </para>
      </note>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00010">
      <title>[H2]Cultural Variations in Social Development and Attachment Security</title>
      <para id="para-00029">
        Attachments was once thought to be formed universally across cultures and to be influenced by evolutionary adaptive traits in our species. However, there are cultural and ecological processes that influence infant <term id="term-00010">attachment</term>. For instance, self-exploration during the first year of life is less common in many non-Western cultures as infants are held or carried for a greater proportion of time (Keller et al., 2009).
        <list list-type="enumerated" number-style="arabic" id="list-00003">
          <item>This is anumbered list</item>
          <item>This is the second number</item>
          <item>This is the third number</item>
        </list>
      </para>
    </section>

    <section class="summary" id="sect-00011">
      <list id="list-00004">
        <item>Involuntary imitation and later voluntary imitation foster an emotional bond between infants and their caregivers</item>
        <item>Infants learn how to process emotions through watching their caregiver and social referencing</item>
        <item>The emotional attachment between an infant and primary caregiver has been theorized to have biological, evolutionary, and cognitive components</item>
        <item>The Strange Situation is an experiment in which different behavioral patterns are identified to characteristic different attachment styles</item>
        <item>Attachment is influenced by cultural and ecological processes and types of attachment at 12 months of age can vary between cultures</item>
      </list>
    </section>

